
  [1mSecond fork: how do browser logs actually reach you.[0m This is the decision that determines whether "debug production
  issues" is satisfied at all — everything else is plumbing around it. In Node, stdout is answer enough. In the browser,
  a log nobody can read is not a log.

  - [1mConsole-only, structured[0m — buys consistent, greppable output and a real logger API; costs nothing; rules out seeing
    anything unless the user sends you a screenshot. Cheap to add a sink to later.
  - [1mIn-memory ring buffer + export[0m — keeps the last N entries and exposes a "download diagnostics" path; buys
    reproduction detail from a cooperating user with no backend and no privacy exposure in transit; still requires a
    human to send you the file.
  - [1mRemote HTTP sink you own[0m — batched POST to an endpoint; buys actual unattended production visibility, which is the
    literal ask; costs an endpoint that doesn't exist yet, a batching/retry/failure policy, and makes redaction
    load-bearing rather than optional.
  - [1mThird-party SDK (Sentry etc.)[0m — buys all of the above plus dashboards for near-zero build cost; costs a vendor
    dependency and a build step this repo doesn't have (no bundler, no [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m), and sends user data to a third
    party.

  My recommendation is [1mconsole-only structured now, with the sink interface designed in[0m — one [38;5;153msinks[39m array the core
  writes to, with the console sink as the only implementation. You get the logger API, the levels, and the redaction
  layer immediately; adding an HTTP or Sentry sink later is a new file, not a rewrite. I'd avoid committing to a remote
  endpoint in the same change that introduces the abstraction, because you don't have an endpoint yet and the privacy
  policy for the login flow deserves its own decision.
